\description{
Combine several independent p-values for each feature into one, by Fisher's or Stouffer's method, optionally weighted and optionally signed by each test's effect.

Each feature is combined on its own by mzLib's \code{PValueCombination}: Fisher (1925),
X = -2 sum ln p against chi-square on 2k degrees of freedom, or Stouffer (1949),
Z = sum w z / sqrt(sum w^2) against the standard normal. mzLib holds both to R's \code{pchisq()} and
\code{qnorm()} to 1e-8 relative. Use it when you have one p-value per study - per dataset, cohort or
laboratory - for the same feature, and want one answer from all of them.
}
\details{
\strong{Direction.} Both rules combine *one-sided* p-values. A two-sided p-value from \code{\link{stats_fit}}
does not say which way the effect went, so two studies that disagree look like two that agree.
Give every test its \code{effect} (its estimate; only the sign is used) and mzLib first turns each p
into the one-sided p-value for effect > 0 (\code{PValueCombination.OneSided}: p/2 for a positive
effect, 1 - p/2 for a negative one), so opposite effects cancel. The combined \code{p_value} then
tests effect > 0; for Stouffer, a \code{p_value} near 1 means a consistently negative effect.

Neither rule estimates an effect size: \code{\link{stats_meta}} does that, from estimates and standard
errors. Neither adjusts across features: send \code{records$p_value} to \code{\link{stats_adjust}}.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_combined_p_values}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per feature in order of first appearance: \code{feature};
    \code{studies}, the tests combined (tests with a finite p-value, after the signed conversion when
    effects were given); \code{omitted}, the feature's tests that were not combined (tests): no
    p-value, or (signed) an effect of 0 or \code{NA}; \code{statistic}, Fisher's X or Stouffer's Z (fisher:
    chi-square statistic on 2 x studies degrees of freedom; stouffer: standard normal deviates),
    \code{NA} when it is not finite; and
    \code{p_value}, a fraction (0 to 1), the upper tail of the statistic, so ONE direction (effect > 0
    when signed), \code{NA} when there is nothing to combine, or a p of 0 and a p of 1 together
    (Stouffer). An \code{NA} statistic with a p-value is an infinite one: \code{p_value} 0 means plus
    infinity (a p-value of 0 among the tests), 1 means minus infinity (Stouffer, a p-value of 1).
  - \code{method}, as you asked; \code{weighted} (Stouffer with weights); \code{signed} (effects were given,
    so every p-value was read as two-sided and converted before combining); \code{line_count} tests
    (lines) sent over every feature; \code{feature_count} features; \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: what the combination assumes, plus any that apply to this input (omitted tests,
    infinite statistics).

}
\section{The trap}{


Two-sided p-values given without their effects are combined as if one-sided, so two studies
whose effects point opposite ways reinforce each other instead of cancelling. Give \code{effect} for
that. mzLib computes no two-sided combined p-value.

}
