\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{method}}{string; required; range \code{fisher | stouffer}. The combination rule. REQUIRED: mzLib has the two as separate methods and prefers neither, so the bridge picks none. fisher is PValueCombination.Fisher, Fisher (1925): X = -2 sum ln p, referred to chi-square with 2k degrees of freedom; it is driven by the smallest p-values and takes no weights. stouffer is PValueCombination.Stouffer, Stouffer et al. (1949): Z = sum w z / sqrt(sum w^2) with z = the standard normal quantile of 1 - p, referred to N(0, 1); it weighs every test.}
\item{\code{tests} (wire \code{--stdin})}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 line of 2 to 4 tab-separated fields}. One test per line, tab-separated: feature, p_value, then optionally weight and effect. No header; blank lines are skipped; a UTF-8 BOM is dropped. A feature's lines need not be adjacent: features are reported in order of first appearance, and each is combined by its own mzLib call. p_value is a fraction in [0, 1]; a blank, NA or NaN p_value is a test that could not be run, which mzLib omits. weight (stouffer only; a positive number, for example the square root of the study's sample size) must be on every line or on none; a blank or NA third field is no weight. effect (the test's estimate, any unit: only its sign is used) must be a fourth field on every line or on none. With effects, each p_value is read as TWO-SIDED and converted by PValueCombination.OneSided to the one-sided p-value for effect > 0 (p/2 for a positive effect, 1 - p/2 for a negative one) before combining; an effect that is 0, blank, NA or NaN has no direction, so mzLib returns NaN and the test is omitted. Write feature, p, a blank weight and the effect to give an effect without a weight.}
}
}
