\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Both rules assume the tests are independent and test the same one-sided hypothesis. Fisher is driven by the smallest p-values; Stouffer weighs every test, and with weights (for example the square root of each study's sample size) lets larger studies count more.
\item p_value is the upper tail of the statistic, so it tests one direction. Two-sided p-values given without their effects are combined as if one-sided, and opposite effects then reinforce each other instead of cancelling: give each test's effect for that.
\item Neither rule estimates an effect size; pool the estimates with stats meta for that. p_value is not adjusted across features; send the p_value column to stats adjust.
\item Signed: p_value tests effect > 0, and for stouffer a p_value near 1 means a consistently negative effect. mzLib computes no two-sided combined p-value. For a negative effect whose two-sided p-value is below about 2e-16, 1 - p/2 rounds to 1, so that test's z is minus infinity and decides a stouffer feature (p_value 1).
}
}
